{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792210000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r3.prompt.md; cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-Local: 2026-10-12 15:10 +07\n# Review r3 (read-only): discovery-order numbering note, round 3, both variants D5 and D6\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering`, branch `numbering-note` (uncommitted edits to `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` and `collab/numbering-probe`). Owner chose discovery-order numbering to replace phase 6a's refinement+search (for simplicity; the rest of the system uses de Bruijn style). Fable's report: `collab/1792190000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise2.report.md`. Your previous review: `collab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`. Check that the three HIGH and two MEDIUM findings are truly fixed (rerun your own counterexamples and your independent 5,000-state generator against the new prototype, in BOTH modes: D5 refusal and D6 residue search); that D6's lemma E (canonicality of the residue search) is sound (try to break it: elements whose trial orders interact, nested conflicts, trials that change which group is least conflicting); that the work/search budgets bound the real cost (include a large-descriptor case and many-cell cases); that the rank spelling `[:u j r]` really preserves the order of 6a's labels; that the section 8 \"Replacing 6a\" list of deletions/rewrites/keeps is accurate against `srvm-phase6a` rows.cljc/rows_test.cljc line numbers; and that the stated coverage (which structures D5 refuses) is exact. Also answer: is D6 still materially simpler than 6a's refinement+search, honestly (net lines, proof obligations, failure modes), or has the note re-grown 6a? Give a recommendation D5 vs D6 with reasons. Foreground only, read-only (no edits to the note or code; write only your findings file). Findings file `collab/1792210000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`, ending with VERDICT: READY_FOR_IMPLEMENTATION or CHANGES_REQUESTED.\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; cat collab/1792190000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise2.report.md; cat docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md; rg --files collab/numbering-probe'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; cat collab/1792190000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise2.report.md; cat docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md; rg --files collab/numbering-probe'","aggregated_output":"I’ll independently rerun the counterexamples, stress the revised numbering rule, and check its proofs and implementation cost against 6a. I’ll keep the worktree read-only.\nThe revised component rule addresses the original aliasing flaw. I’m now checking whether local runs remain canonical when sharing crosses several containers, and whether memoization supports the claimed work bound. The proof needs more scrutiny than the new fixtures alone provide.\nThe original counterexamples now produce matching roots, and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` numbers successfully. I found a spec/prototype discrepancy: D5 says a conflict in any local run causes refusal, while key-building local runs can retain ties and let the enclosing run resolve them later. I’m testing a small witness and the memoization bound.\nMy independent 5,000-state run finished with no root mismatches across two renamed and shuffled copies per state: 4,527 numbered and 473 refused. A targeted test found another gap: decode correctly translates a root D5 refusal, but a refusal in an install child escapes as `:yin.k/non-portable`. The revised implementation is also substantially larger than the first draft’s simplicity estimate.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 20:46:32 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 03:46:32 Asia/Bangkok\n\nThe original counterexamples are fixed. My independent 5,000-state search found no enumeration-dependent roots, but the revised note still has specification, decode, and work-bound defects. It also no longer delivers the promised simplification.\n\nReview was read-only at HEAD `1bb2638cfcf00e509f34bf463e3083e51d593697`. No files were edited, staged, or committed. Timestamps use the available clock, which precedes the supplied Created-GMT.\n\nBelow, `note` means [the numbering note](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md), `probe` means [discovery.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj), and `rows` means phase 6a’s `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`.\n\n| Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **High** | note:254; probe:262 | D5 requires lift refusal at a conflicting fixpoint of **any run**. However, key-building local runs return incomplete spellings without propagating refusal. `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` encounters such a conflict locally, then numbers and decodes successfully. | Distinguish speculative key runs from runs whose completeness is required. State precisely when local D5 becomes a lift refusal, and update F4, lemma D, and amendments accordingly. |\n| **High** | probe:419, 425; note:280 | Decode translates root D5, but an install child’s D5 escapes from `emit-node*` as `:yin.k/non-portable`, with path `[:child 15]`. | Wrap the entire discovery recomputation, including child emission, in the narrow translation specified by the note. Prefix the receiver path with the carried root id. |\n| **High** | note:290–306 | `P` counts expanded positions, but the claimed bound counts bytes too. With identical structure and fixed `P,d,n,m`, enlarging a stream descriptor from 128 to 65,536 characters increased encoded key bytes from 3,509 to 592,208. The memoization argument also ignores its counter dependency and different simulated numbering contexts. | Include expanded byte volume, including repeated body-label bytes, in the bound. Prove a bound on distinct memo contexts; monotonicity within one run does not establish it across local runs. |\n| **Medium** | note:423 | A2 says the procedure “never reads” host enumeration order. D4 explicitly enters equal components in host order. | Say the emitted commitment and acceptance decision are invariant under host enumeration, with the permitted D4 use justified by lemma C. |\n| **Medium** | note:635–647 | The prototype’s schema-and-algorithm region occupies roughly 330 physical lines; lines 67–364 alone contain 262 nonblank, noncomment lines. This does not support “about 230 lines with the schema helpers,” nor the assertion that adoption costs one review round. | Replace the optimistic size and review estimates with an implementation-based estimate, including work accounting and child refusal translation. |\n\n**1. Previous counterexamples and mismatches**\n\nI reran the prototype using `java -cp` and the existing classpath, avoiding launcher cache writes.\n\n| Case | Independent rerun |\n|---|---|\n| `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`, both enumerations | X=1, Y=2, Z=3; identical roots |\n| `{X X, Y Z}`, both enumerations | X=1, Y=2, Z=3; identical roots |\n| `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | Total numbering; reversal and renaming preserve the root |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | Numbers successfully; reversal and renaming preserve the root |\n| Root D5 decode translation | Correctly returns `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` |\n| Install-child D5 decode translation | **Still incorrect**, as reported above |\n| Integer `[:n k]`, parked structural references, K frames | Previous prototype mismatches fixed |\n| Install children using discovery numbering | Fixed for successful children |\n| Totality | An unreachable declared cursor cell raises the expected defect, with `{:missing [:a], :extra []}` |\n\nThe author’s complete 2,300-state run reproduced its reported statistics. Existing 6a row tests also passed: **21 tests, 324 assertions, zero failures or errors**.\n\n**2. Independent adversarial search and proof review**\n\nMy generator used ten families of incidence structures rather than the author’s recursive value generator: overlapping groups, randomized pair relations, aliased map keys, repeated collections, nested components, ordered anchors, and mixtures of equal and unequal bodies. It used 2–8 cursor cells. Of the 5,000 states, 250 included an install child.\n\nFor every state, I compared the original with **two independently renamed copies**, each permuting every set, map, and census. Renaming and traversal were implemented separately from the author’s transformation.\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---:|\n| Random states | 5,000 |\n| Numbered | 4,527 |\n| Refused | 473 |\n| Root/refusal-outcome mismatches | **0** |\n| Exceptions during those comparisons | **0** |\n| Decode round trips in the first 300 states | 193 successful |\n| Decode resource refusals in that subset | 80 |\n| Other decode failures in that subset | **0** |\n| Cached versus cache-cleared local runs | 150 states, **0 differences** |\n\nI initially generated some containers with duplicate decoded values despite different listed representations. I corrected that generator artifact before the reported 5,000-state run.\n\nNo additional memoization poisoning or accepted-state canonicality counterexample was found. Equal element keys can still conceal sharing with other elements, but the component construction correctly accounts for that sharing in the cases tested.\n\nThe two targeted failures have these exact constructions:\n\n```clojure\n(require '[probe.discovery :as p]\n         '[yin.vm.ucf.rows :as r]\n         '[yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as t])\n\n(def b (t/cursor-cell-body :s 0))\n(def c #(vector :cursor-cell %))\n(def v #(vector :row (r/node :vec (vec %))))\n(def s #(vector :row (r/node :set (vec %))))\n\n(def cycle-value\n  (s [(v [(c :a) (c :b)])\n      (v [(c :b) (c :a)])]))\n\n;; A key-building local run reaches D5, but lift succeeds.\n(def local-d5-witness\n  (t/task-node [[:a b] [:b b]]\n               (v [(s [cycle-value]) (c :a)])))\n\n;; A valid carried child reaches D5 during discovery recomputation.\n(def cycle-task\n  (t/task-node [[:a b] [:b b]] cycle-value))\n\n(def child\n  (r/node :task (assoc (:slots cycle-task) 3 :child)))\n\n(def parent\n  (r/node :task\n          (assoc (:slots (t/task-node [] [:lit nil]))\n                 12 [(r/node :install\n                             ['a :running nil {} child])])))\n\n(p/discovery-numbering local-d5-witness)\n;; {:numbering {:a :yin.k/cc-1, :b :yin.k/cc-2}}\n\n(p/error #(p/decode* (r/emit-numbered parent {})))\n;; :yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\n;; :yin.k/kind   :unresolved-cursor-cells\n;; :yin.k/path   [:child 15]\n```\n\n`local-d5-witness` also decodes successfully. Its root is:\n\n```text\n9600fe01f89d891085e7352c81da45d022e9088b0b897cce46dad755848485b5\n```\n\nInstalling a child **inside a tied guest element** is outside the carrier schema: `:install` and `:task` are not permitted value-row kinds (`rows:404, 531`). I therefore tested tied values within legal install children rather than treating that malformed shape as a numbering counterexample.\n\nThe line-by-line proof assessment is:\n\n| Passage | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Lemma A, note:323–331 | Reading structure, bodies, and corresponding numbers is appropriate. The nesting induction needs to cover component local runs explicitly. Its appeal to C is reasonable only with a stated simultaneous induction and decreasing measure. |\n| Lemma B, note:333–334 | Correct by construction. It establishes key equality, not structural interchangeability. |\n| Lemma C, note:336–353 | The substantive repair is sound under its assumptions: unnumbered-cell sets are disjoint; complete indexed spellings preserve alias topology; group numbers preserve group membership; block swaps therefore preserve the configuration. The completeness requirement is essential. |\n| Lemma D, note:355–359 | Conflict incidence is invariant. The completeness argument depends on A. Which local conflicts actually cause refusal remains inconsistent between D5 and the prototype. |\n| Theorem, note:361–371 | Plausible after fixing refusal semantics and formalizing the mutual induction. Testing found no contrary accepted-state result. |\n| Termination, note:378–384 | Root-run progress is credible. “One level down” should identify the decreasing unordered-container measure despite synthetic component wrappers. It does not prove the advertised memoized work bound. |\n\n**3. Simplicity audit**\n\nThe revised design is **not materially simpler or cheaper to review** than 6a.\n\nMy estimate, in existing `rows.cljc` formatting, is approximately **250–330 added or replacement lines and 135–150 deleted lines**, yielding roughly **100–195 net added lines**, excluding tests. Existing helpers reduce the prototype size, but portable budget accounting, refusal paths, and complete decode translation add work back.\n\n| Aspect | 6a refinement/search | Revised discovery |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main proof obligations | Invariant signatures/positions; terminating refinement; exhaustive branch set with invariant least commitment | Canonical recursive keys; tie bookkeeping; complete component isomorphisms; invariant refusal; additionally memo correctness and recursive progress |\n| Refusal class | Resource exhaustion under documented budgets; no D5 structural exclusion | Resource exhaustion **plus** uncertified sharing components |\n| Lost coverage | Seven-cell fixture exceeds the label budget | Also refuses simple 2-cycles and 3-cycles that 6a numbers |\n| Principal failure modes | Growing labels, exhaustive search cost, budget exhaustion | Context-sensitive memo bugs, incomplete local spellings, worklist/group bookkeeping, premature refusal, child exception leakage |\n| Cost advantage | Poor worst-case search | Avoids exhaustive search, but its claimed polynomial accounting remains unproved |\n\nThe lemma count understates discovery’s burden: lemma B is almost tautological, while A and C carry substantially more machinery than their count suggests.\n\nThe revised note remains valuable for its complete position inventory, alias-preserving local spelling, totality assertion, distinction between canonical order and host enumeration, expanded-DAG accounting, and narrow decode translation. Keep those findings and the counterexample fixtures. If 6a remains, the new D5 translation is a future requirement rather than a currently necessary change.\n\n**4. Grounding, amendments, pins, and portability**\n\nI checked more than twelve cited locations. The scratch `rows.cljc` and `rows_test.cljc` matched their `srvm-phase6a` versions by checksum.\n\n| Reference checked | Result |\n|---|---|\n| rows:37–57 | Position inventory matches the schema |\n| rows:140–145 | Numeric census ordering confirmed |\n| rows:148–156 | Final emitted collection ordering confirmed |\n| rows:185–189 | Independent child numbering confirmed |\n| rows:200–202 | Budget keys confirmed |\n| rows:212–230 | Label-size checker confirmed |\n| rows:252–255 | Initial labels confirmed |\n| rows:257–284 | Content encoding confirmed |\n| rows:285–325 | Position collection confirmed |\n| rows:326–354 | Classes, refinement, and search confirmed |\n| rows:570–575 | Decode recomputation and comparison confirmed |\n| rows:576–578 | Frame validation follows numbering |\n| rows_test:28, 159, 171, 206 | Named fixtures/tests confirmed |\n| rows_test:307, 331, 392, 406 | Pins, numeric census, seven-cell and large-input tests confirmed |\n| engine:227, 311, 557, 2312, 2595 | Revised citations correct against the named branch |\n\nA1–A10 target the intended passages, and the integer encoding, child spelling change, diagnostic counts, and revised body label are explicit. A2 still contradicts D4’s permitted host-order use; the refusal descriptions inherit the D5 ambiguity.\n\nThese pinned BLAKE3 digests were recomputed:\n\n| Fixture | Digest |\n|---|---|\n| Two-cell fixture | `fab223d9b3540fdce0ccf2132f8a4041917a158a32295470a4936754742d3ac4` |\n| Review A | `65ba4530bd8908e734a8be4fcc594d2989c5a8a7d1017779ef8f62991df38dab` |\n| Review B | `409548a71f080cae6f3f66911409370d83a5df9e928135d95c497e9b770d9328` |\n| Nested sets | `18b70de6d4533d5ca797464568b5dd7812ca4b2f36a1e8fb64ef823a2eb3da45` |\n| Repeated set | `92747c0f27e9c64876267b19dafa34edd359822341eca32d6067e199a926f116` |\n\nFor portability, a guest map containing a nil key and a nil-valued entry numbered and decoded successfully. A 20,000-element cursor-ref vector numbered and emitted successfully. Continuation §12 correctly retains `contains?` membership, `mapv`/`into`/`reduce` for large sequences, and `.cljc` fixture data instead of whitespace-sensitive EDN strings. **Node and Dart execution were not performed**; the scratch prototype’s private-var access is not portable.\n\n**Recommendation: keep 6a’s refinement design with its documented resource limitation, retaining the revised note’s useful inventory, counterexamples, totality requirement, and decode guidance while deferring adoption of discovery-order numbering.**\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-12 06:55:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 13:55:00 Asia/Bangkok\n\n(Host clock as read by `date -u` at completion.)\n\n# Architect report: discovery-order numbering note, round 3\n\nEdited: `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`\n(rewritten, 980 lines), the prototype\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (934 lines) with its run\nlog `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`, and two probe helpers\n(`collab/numbering-probe/java.args`, the launcher's cached classpath with\nabsolute paths, and `run.sh`). Nothing staged or committed. The 6a code\nwas read from branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`); the probe's copies of\n`rows.cljc`, `rows_test.cljc` and `frame.cljc` were re-diffed against the\nbranch and are identical. 6a's own tests pass in the probe project\n(21 tests, 324 assertions).\n\nOne environment note: the `clojure` launcher (and bare `java`) needed an\napproval this non-interactive session could not give; the JVM ran\nthrough `mise exec -- java @java.args clojure.main …` on the launcher's\ncached classpath, foreground, twice (before and after one diagnostic\nchange). All numbers below are from the second run, which is the log.\n\n## Per item of the brief\n\n**1. HIGH, D5 semantics (key runs swallow conflicts).** Decided and\nspecified: only the *root run* and *component runs* (runs whose\ncompleteness D4 requires) refuse on a conflict; a *key run* (made to\nsort one element) is speculative, never refuses, and spells its residue\n`[:tie …]` in the key, which lemma A shows is canonical. The note's §0\nnames the three kinds of local run, F4 restates the predicate (\"the root\nrun, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which …\"),\nD5 says it, lemma D is rewritten around it, and A1/A4 carry it. The\nwitness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` is now a fixture: `A cc-1, B cc-2`, root\n`9600fe01…` (equal to the review's digest), one root over 24 renamings\nplus shuffles, decodes. The prototype already behaved this way; the\nspec was wrong, and the diagnostic is now also host-independent (a\nrefusal names the least conflicting group's path and the sorted cells of\nevery conflicting component; the seven-cell state reports all seven).\n\n**2. HIGH, install-child refusal escaping decode.** The prototype's\n`decode*` now wraps the whole discovery recomputation, root and install\nchildren (`emit*`, which numbers children inside `emit-node*`), in the\nnarrow translation; a lift-side child refusal carries path\n`[12 <install name> …]` (a path is threaded through `emit-node*`), and\nthe receiver reports `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with\npath `[<root id> 12 a 15]`. Verified on a parent whose install `'a`\nholds the 2-cycle: lift refuses `[12 a 15]`; the `emit-numbered` carrier\ndecodes to the undecodable kind with the prefixed path. The note's\n\"Validation and decode\" paragraph and A4 say \"the whole of that call …\nroot and every install child\"; §8 points at `emit-node :185-189` and\n`decode* :570-575`.\n\n**3. HIGH, work bound.** Chose the brief's third option plus a\nstructural fix: the polynomial claim is dropped; `:work` is an explicit\nbudget charged per position visited, per position spelled and per byte\ncopied into a spelling, `:search` per D6 trial, both refusing\n`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]` as 6a's `:label-bytes` did;\nthe memo is called an optimization with no context bound claimed. The\nstructural fix: a provisional cell is spelled `[:u j r]` with `r` the\nbody *rank* among the census's distinct body labels, not the label. Rank\norder equals label order (CBOR is prefix-free, so a comparison stops at\nthe same position with the same sign), so no numbering changes; every\npin of rounds 1 and 2 reproduced. Measured on a 4-cell state: label\nspelling 4364 key bytes at a 128-character descriptor and 920118 at\n65536; rank spelling 1396 at both. The prototype now charges the budgets:\n`{:work 1}` refuses at `emit` and propagates untranslated through\n`decode`; `{:search 1}` refuses the 3-cycle under D6. Largest `:work`\nover 2300 generated states: 1470 (6a's default is 200000).\n\n**4. MEDIUM, A2 and the estimates.** A2 now says the emitted commitment\nand the acceptance decision are invariant under host enumeration, names\nD4's entry of equal-encoding components as the procedure's one use of\nhost order, justified by lemma C. §7's estimate is implementation-based:\nprototype schema-to-procedure region about 330 nonblank lines (about 30\nof them D6, diagnostics, tracing, measurement); in place, reusing 6a's\nhelpers, 250–300 added, 135–150 deleted, about 100–160 net; the review's\nindependent 100–195 is quoted beside it. Review cost: one implementation\nround and one review round, reading §3 and §8.\n\n**5. IMPORTANT, coverage regression.** F4 states exactly which\nstructures D5 refuses: two or more equal-body cursor cells held *only*\nthrough guest sets or maps, in equal-keyed elements each mentioning more\nthan one of them (the directed 2-cycle and 3-cycle, which 6a numbers and\nwhich are interchangeable; the path and the seven-cell state, which are\nnot). Whether realistic tasks hit them is stated as unmeasured (no v3\ncorpus), with the structural argument (cells are created into\nregisters; a D5 state needs guest code to build a set of cursor tuples\non one stream at one position and drop every other reference) and the\ngenerator rate (35 of 2300). Rather than leave the regression, the note\nspecifies **D6, residue search**: at a conflicting component, one trial\nper element of its least conflicting group (that element entered first\nwhen its container is keyed), run to completion, least spelling adopted,\n`:search` charged per trial; lemma E proves it canonical; consequence 6\nand the run show it moves no D1–D4 id. Prototyped behind\n`:residue-search`: the 2-cycle and 3-cycle reach **6a's exact roots**\n(`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`) in 2 and 3 trials; the path `726a9fbb…`, the\nloop plus 2-cycle `858a891e…` (`L cc-1`), the seven-cell state\n`d76d787a…` in 11 trials and 488 work (6a refused it on label bytes);\neach one root over 24 renamings plus shuffles. The note recommends D6\nand keeps the D5 variant fully specified (kind, translation, tests) as\nthe owner's alternative; §10 question 1 is the choice.\n\n**6. \"Replacing 6a\".** §8 lists, with `srvm-phase6a` line numbers, what\n`rows.cljc` deletes (`check-label-bytes!` 212–230; inside\n`canonical-numbering`: labels 252–255, `ce` 257–284, `positions`\n285–325, `classes` 326–330, `refine` 331–337, `search` 338–354, the\nchecked wrappers 245–251; the `:label-bytes` key, and `:search` in the\nD5 variant), rewrites (`canonical-numbering` same contract; `emit-node`\npath; `decode*` 570–575 wrap; docstring), and keeps (everything else,\nnamed), and what `rows_test.cljc` keeps (every fixture and test, named,\nincluding `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`) and rewrites (four tests,\nwith their new names, assertions and pins in both variants). Every\ncounterexample of §6 is a fixture in acceptance items 12–16. Portability\nnotes for the rewrite are included (no `for` over long seqs, no `@#'`,\n`(assoc {} …)`).\n\n## The rerun (both modes, foreground)\n\n| Search | D5 variant | D6 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2000 random value states (nested sets, `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`-style pairs, cursor-cell map keys, tied elements, cells aliased across containers) | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (14 witnessed non-interchangeable, 11 not distinguished), 0 invariance failures, 1783 decoded, 0 decode failures, 192 at 6a's own recompute budget, max work 1254 | 2000 numbered, 0 refused, 0 invariance failures, 1808 decoded, 0 failures, 192 at 6a's budget, max trials 13, max work 1470 |\n| 200 frame-bearing (act window, K-frame window, environments, parked state) | 192 / 8 (5 / 3), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget | 200 / 0, 183 decoded, max trials 5 |\n| 100 with install children | 98 / 2 (0 / 2), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget | 100 / 0, 76 decoded, max trials 4 |\n\nEvery fixture root of rounds 1 and 2 reproduced, with rank spelling and\nwith D6 on. New pins: `9600fe01…` (witness); under D6 `0fe898cc…`,\n`0b181cd4…`, `726a9fbb…`, `858a891e…`, `d76d787a…`. Still refused in the\nD5 variant: the 2-cycle, 3-cycle, path, loop plus 2-cycle and seven-cell\nstate (named honestly in F4 and §6); under D6 nothing is refused except\nby budget. The \"192 at 6a's budget\" column is the probe decoding through\n6a's own `decode*`, which still runs 6a's search; a probe limitation,\nnot a numbering outcome, as before.\n\n## Unverified or disputed\n\n- No review finding is disputed; all five reproduced and are addressed.\n- Three-host runs, kondo, and the real codec's budget charging: not run\n  (documents and a JVM scratch prototype only). The prototype's decode\n  check still runs after 6a's `validate-frames`; the real codec places it\n  at step 6 (acceptance item 20, test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`).\n- The real-state rate of D5 conflicts is unmeasured (no v3 corpus); D6\n  makes the question moot for correctness and leaves it as a cost\n  question (trials observed: 2–13).\n- The cost bound is enforced by budget, not proved; `:work` and\n  `:search` defaults are 6a's (200000, 10000) and were never approached.\n- The D6 trial order (\"entered first when its container is keyed\") is a\n  definition, not the only possible one; any deterministic choice is\n  canonical by lemma E, so an implementer must follow the note's, not\n  invent another.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**READY for an implementation brief**, cut from §8, in the D6 variant\n(recommended) or the D5 variant, once the owner answers §10 question 1.\nRemaining risks: a canonicality bug in the port (mitigated by the\ngenerator of item 18 in CI, the decode round trip, and the proof); the\n`for`-over-32-elements and `@#'` traps when porting to `.cljc`; the\nunproved cost bound (budgeted, never approached in 2300 states); and the\nunmeasured real-state conflict rate (a cost question only under D6).\n# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Written 2026-10-12\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the\nproblem is not that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity\nis local\", and the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by\n**discovery order** in a canonical traversal. Revised twice the same day\nafter independent reviews (gpt-6.1-sol,\n`collab/1792140000000-…findings.md` and `collab/1792170000000-…findings.md`).\nRound 2 repaired the orbit lemma (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested tie groups\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 3 (this text) fixes what the second review\nfound: which local conflicts refuse (§2 D5, witness\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`), the install-child refusal escaping decode, the\nwork bound (now an explicit budget, with body labels spelled by rank so\nkey bytes no longer grow with a stream descriptor), the A2 wording, the\nsize estimate, and the coverage regression against 6a (2-cycles and\n3-cycles), for which §2 D6 specifies and §6 measures the repair. §8 lists\nwhat an implementation deletes, rewrites and keeps in 6a. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\n(the carrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\n(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that\ncode (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\n\n**In one line:** number cursor cells in the order a canonical traversal\nmeets them (D1–D2); order the elements of a guest set or map by a key\nthat is the spelling of a speculative local run over the element (so\nalias patterns such as `[X X]` versus `[Y Z]` are part of the key);\ndefer equal keys and re-examine them to a fixpoint (D3); at a fixpoint,\nlink tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell into components\nand enter the component of least complete encoding (D4); a component\nholding two equal-keyed elements of one container is the one case this\ncannot certify: refuse it (D5), or resolve it by a bounded residue search\nover that component alone (D6, recommended). Proof in §3, budget in §2\n\"Work\", results in §6, the cut against 6a in §8.\n\n## 0. Vocabulary used below\n\n- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of\n  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed\n  order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a\n  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an\n  accident of the host. The procedure reads it in exactly one place, D4's\n  entry of components of equal encoding, where lemma C shows it cannot\n  change the result.\n- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`\n  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).\n- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to\n  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is\n  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.\n- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure\n  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root\n  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its\n  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one item from\n  the enclosing run's current numbering. There are three kinds of local\n  run, and the distinction decides what refuses (D5):\n  - a **key run** is a local run over one element, made to compute the\n    element's sort key; it is *speculative*: it never refuses, and\n    whatever it cannot resolve (a tie, a conflict) is spelled in its\n    key as `[:tie …]`;\n  - a **component run** is a local run over a component's synthetic\n    `[:component …]` row (D4); it must be *complete*, else D5;\n  - a **trial** (D6 only) is a component run that enters one chosen\n    element before its siblings.\n- A **body label** is `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`,\n  a cursor cell's census body as plain data; its **rank** is its index\n  among the census's distinct body labels in bytewise order.\n\n## 1. Findings\n\n**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\n(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\ncursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\n\n| Position | Order | Canonical index |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\n| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\n| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\n| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |\n| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \"sorted\") |\n| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |\n| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\n| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\n| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\n\nSo the only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest\n`:map` entries. Both reviews confirmed the inventory complete. The\nexample state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one\ncursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's\nenvironment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`\npending's `cursor-cell-C` slot.\n\n**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\nstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\nit occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a\nfixed order, so \"the n-th cursor cell met\" already encodes the first\nposition of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned\nnumber and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)\nexisted only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery\norder is a total order by construction.\n\n**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that\ncarries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor\ncell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike. The\nrepaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\") numbers the cursor cells *inside* an\nelement provisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to\nthe enclosing numbering: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells\n`[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology inside an element is part of its sort\nkey, as a de Bruijn index is part of a term. The key of an element is the\nspelling produced by a *key run* over it, which applies the same rules\nrecursively (nested containers sorted, nested ties deferred and\nre-examined, D4 inside) and spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`.\nEqual keys form a **tie group**, deferred to the run's worklist and\nre-examined to a fixpoint (D3) as other positions assign numbers. A\nprovisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so\na key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length\nof a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\").\n\n**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's\nfixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur\nonly inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an\nunnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\n**component**. Two cases:\n- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\n  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in\n  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,\n  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by\n  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\n  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).\n  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and\n  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\n- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then\n  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be\n  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component\n  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a\n  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`\n  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.\n  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`\n  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is\n  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets\n  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)\n  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the\n  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).\n  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is\n  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.\n  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the\n  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which\n  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected\n  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*\n\n  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more\n  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,\n  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in\n  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The\n  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed\n  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the\n  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,\n  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell\n  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and\n  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under\n  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts\n  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector\n  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole\n  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in\n  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no\n  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census\n  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural\n  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register\n  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a\n  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on\n  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of\n  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\n  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).\n  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,\n  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:\n  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over\n  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a\n  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in\n  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle\n  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.\n  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6\n  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.\n\n**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\nrefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\nof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\ncensus) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2\n\"Work\".\n\n**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\ncursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\nmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\nsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\nframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\nstate) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself\nrenamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance\nfailures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6\nrefuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default\n200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1\nand 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);\nthe second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes\nof one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label\nspelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character\ndescriptor.\n\n**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n\n## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n\nInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\ncells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\ntask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\nrefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\nrule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n\n**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\ntraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\nnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\nrun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\nenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the\ncell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,\nso no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,\nand the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor\ncell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else\nchanges from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`\nexpanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\n`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's\n`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install\nentry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides\nan order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are\nspelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register\norder; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its\nelements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,\nwhere a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as\n`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are\nbytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free.\n\n**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\nrow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\nkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\nspecifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is\nnot entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a\n**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering\nat the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that\none numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\nequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\norder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\ncontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\nan entry's key before its value.\n\n**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\ncell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\ncounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\nlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\nenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\nno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\ncontents table.\n\n**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\nre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\nthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\nre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\ncell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\nit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)\njoins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is\none worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred\ngroups from every depth.\n\n**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\nlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\ncell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\nfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\nso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component\nby a component run over the synthetic ordered row\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral\nsequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that\ngroup, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a\nsynthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be\ncomplete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it\nreaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the\ncomponents by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:\neach of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,\nin that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's\nnumbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,\nlemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).\nRemove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group\nwhose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\ncarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\nhost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n\n**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when\nit holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the\nroot run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component\nrun of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed\n`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered\ncursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the\ndiagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run\nnever refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing\nrefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is\na resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\n**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A\nconflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting\ngroup of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each\n`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`\nrow in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before\nits siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local\nindices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to\ncompletion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The\ncomponent's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the\ncomponent adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are\nisomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no\nstructural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the\nresource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not\ndefined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts\ntrials; without it, the key is deleted.\n\n**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\nBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\nbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\ndefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\nrefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\n\n**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\nlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\nrestricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and\n(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and\nmust return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the\nprototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of\nanother element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo\nholds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no\nunaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is\nclaimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound.\n\n**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\nthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\non mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\n(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*\n(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\n(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`\nkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the\ncarried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);\na resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\nitself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\nalready checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an\ninstall child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path\n`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the\nprototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The\nvalidator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before\n`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the\ntranslation has nothing to translate and is omitted.\n\n**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the\nround-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream\ndescriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and\nthat memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.\nThe procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:\nthe `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,\nevery local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per\nbyte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the\ncopy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the\nstructural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the\ncarried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the\nresource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`\nwas. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,\nso the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the\ncarried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:\na key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of\nthe number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key\nbytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound\nwithout memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and\n`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,\n`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor\ncells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than\nthe analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`\nunits, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default\n200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The\n`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels.\n\n## 3. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\n**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\nother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\norder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\n(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\nThe proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of\nunnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):\na key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more\nunnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested\nunordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so\nthe lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns.\n\n**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\nof a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks\n(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and\nnumbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested\ncontainers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,\n(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,\ncovered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a\nconflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is\na function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under\ncorresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order\nof a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism.\n\n**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\nconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\n\n**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\nof one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let\n`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise\ndisjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an\nunnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).\nEqual, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ i₁ …]` rows are a faithful\nspelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by\nindex and body rank, groups by sequence number, a group's several\nelements as a sorted set), so they exhibit, for each pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a\nbijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the same structure\ngroup by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives the cells of\n`C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index; in order\n`σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The block-swap\nbijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an isomorphism of the\ntwo resulting configurations: numbers agree by construction, structures\nagree because the swapped components are isomorphic and nothing outside\nthem mentions their cells, and the worklists agree because the same\ngroups remain, with the same elements removed. ∎\n\n**Lemma D (the D5 verdict is invariant).** Whether the root run, or a\ncomponent run it requires, reaches a fixpoint with a conflicting\ncomponent depends only on which tied elements share unnumbered cursor\ncells at that fixpoint, which is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism (lemma A gives the ties, the sharing relation is structural);\nand whether a component run is complete is the same question one level\ndown, by the induction. Key runs do not enter the verdict: their\nconflicts are spelled, not refused, and the spelling is canonical (lemma\nA iii).\n\n**Lemma E (D6 trials).** At a conflicting component the set `E` of\nelements of its least conflicting group is a function of the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism (lemma D names the component and its\ngroups canonically). Each trial is a deterministic function of (the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism, the chosen element), by the induction\non the trial run, which has strictly fewer unnumbered cells than the\nenclosing run (its forced element holds at least one, being linked into\nthe component by a shared unnumbered cell). So the multiset of trial\nspellings is canonical and its least member is canonical. Two trials\nwith equal least spellings have produced isomorphic numberings of the\ncomponent (faithful spelling, as in lemma C), so adopting either yields\nisomorphic configurations. ∎\n\n**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the\nsame: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical\nrows. *Proof.* By the induction above. D1 and D2 decisions are functions\nof the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only repeats D1 on\ndeferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the least run is\ncanonical (lemma A on component encodings, which are complete by D5/D6)\nand its only listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant\n(lemma D); D6 is invariant (lemma E). Emission spells cursor cells by\nnumber only and sorts every emitted unordered slot by canonical bytes\nunder the final numbering (carrier §5), so isomorphic final\nconfigurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\nConsequences:\n\n1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:\n   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or\n   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the\n   point lemma C covers.\n2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step\n   3), so every traversal is finite. In any run each D3 round that is not\n   the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least one\n   cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; each local run is over an\n   item with fewer unordered containers nested below it than its\n   spawning run's item, or (a trial) with fewer unnumbered cells, so the\n   induction measure of §3 decreases and there are finitely many local\n   runs. The budget of §2 \"Work\" is what bounds the count.\n3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\n   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\n4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\n   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of\n   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one\n   position get two numbers.\n5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\n   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\n   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.\n6. *D6 moves no D1–D4 id.* Trials run only where D4 found a conflict,\n   which without D6 is a refusal; every numbering D1–D4 assign is\n   identical with and without D6 (§6 checks the pins both ways).\n\n## 4. Amendments\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §2 above (vocabulary, the spelling, D1–D6, totality, work). Keep the\nsection's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is\ndefined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering is defined\nas the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is a function\nof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\nsets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the\ntraversal cannot make by itself, between two equal-keyed elements of one\nguest set or map that are linked by shared unnumbered cursor cells at a\nfixpoint of the root run or of a component run, is resolved by a search\nconfined to that component (D6) [or, in the D5 variant: is refused (D5),\nnot searched; the refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and\ncan be narrowed later without changing any numbering assigned today].\"\nState in step 2's replacement that `[:n k]` and `[:u j r]` carry exact\nCBOR integers, that `r` is the body rank, and that `[:child]` replaces\n`[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (proof in the numbering note, §3); the procedure's one use of host\norder, entering components of equal encoding in the order listed, cannot\nchange the rows (lemma C), and a receiver never decides validity by its\nown enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a\nlift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check [D5 variant: ; a receiver that reaches\nD5 refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`].\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6\nare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\nof an emitted row\" with \"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are\nused only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of\nan emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the\nbound is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" D5 variant: add\n\"`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached\nin the root or in a child, translated from the recomputation's lift-side\nrefusal of that kind only, its path prefixed by the carried root id\";\nand in the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 add\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds, change\n\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n`:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds …, `:metadata` and\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\", and in §11 \"two lift kinds and two\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" to \"three … and three …\". With D6\nthese counts are unchanged.\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth, traversal-and-spelling work and (D6) residue-search\ntrials for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions and copied bytes, so\nspellings are paid for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls\nback to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for\nthe canonical traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\"\n→ \"as defined by discovery order [D5 variant: , and the D5 refusal\nclass]\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\ntraversal)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \"numbered by the draft's §6\nprocedure\" with \"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \"Draft §6 is\ninvariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode\nan incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's\nbody label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6\nas amended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\".\n(ii) \"Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the\nparked table) are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise\n(draft §6 step 2), never by host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps\nare entered in the bytewise order of their elements' spellings, and\n`:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in the byte order of their\nstructural keys (draft §6 D1), never in host enumeration order\". (iii)\nLeave \"never derived from traversal order\" but qualify it: \"never\nderived from *host* traversal order (the canonical traversal of draft §6\nD1 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–22\nof §9 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \"minted by the emitter (stable within\none lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\" remains true.\n\n## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\nmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\n(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id\ncounter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts\nas `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the\n`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the\nsealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,\ncounter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not\nstructure:\n\n- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:\n  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,\n  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes\n  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name\n  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two\n  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at\n  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A\n  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root\n  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead\n  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.\n- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\n  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\n  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\n  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\n- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\n  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\n  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\n  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\n  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\n  relative to its state.\n\nDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\nidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\nn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\nposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\nids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the\nonly kind proposed.\n\n## 6. Prototype\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\nscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D6 as\none schema-driven run procedure (root run, key runs, component runs and\ntrials share the code; a local run differs only in its number tag, in\nspelling residues as `[:tie]` and, for a trial, in its forced element),\nmemoized as §2 describes, charging `:work` and `:search` as §2 \"Work\"\ndescribes, with the totality assertion, an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure, a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and then wraps the whole discovery\nrecomputation (root and children) in the narrow translation, and two\nswitches: `:residue-search` (D6; off is the D5 variant) and\n`:permissive` (diagnostic: a conflict is resolved by one host-first\ntrial, to test whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable). Run\nfrom the probe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private\nhelpers); the `clojure` launcher was not available to this session, so\nthe run below used the launcher's cached classpath directly:\n\n```\njava @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 3 (full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`;\n6a's own tests pass unchanged in the same project):\n\n| State | Discovery order (D5 variant) | With D6 | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same | same root |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | same | root `2d9873a8…` |\n| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | component rule, root `07eda35d…` | same | same root |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | same | same roots |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | same | `71543fe2…` / same |\n| **review A** `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `65ba4530…` (the review's digest) | same | `387f294a…` |\n| **review B** `{X X, Y Z}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `409548a7…` (the review's digest) | same | `be62ba43…` |\n| **review C** `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | total numbering, inner set listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`; root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | numbered (two components, each one element per group), root `92747c0f…` | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2`, root `7dc3d5c4…` | same | `a850fce6…` |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3, W cc-4`, root `6ea03f9b…` | same | `e61d3709…` |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | keys differ (two cursor cells against four); root `b35b8e12…` | same | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| **review r2 witness** `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | key run conflicts, root run numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`; root `9600fe01…` (the review's digest); 1 root over 24 renamings plus shuffles; decodes | same | `01933121…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | `L cc-1`, then refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[A B]` | `L cc-1, A cc-2, B cc-3`, 2 trials, root `858a891e…`, 1 root over 24 | root `55ff168e…` (`L cc-3`) |\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 1 root over 24 shuffles (interchangeable, not certified) | `A cc-1, B cc-2`, 2 trials, root `0fe898cc…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` | refused; permissive 1 root | 3 trials, root `0b181cd4…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 2 roots (not interchangeable) | 2 trials, root `726a9fbb…`, 1 root over 24 | `76117652…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused; permissive 1 root over 24 (the 4 of round 2 came from listed-order entry) | 11 trials, 488 `:work`, root `d76d787a…`, 1 root over 24; decode through 6a's `decode*` stops at 6a's own `[:label-bytes]` (probe limitation) | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same | same root |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `0b9ef61e…`; decodes | same | `fd3513dd…` |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1`, `x cc-2`, `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `ff124bfc…`; decodes | same | `e1558410…` |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; one child row shared; root `9544fcd7…`; decodes | same | — |\n| **install child holding the 2-cycle** | lift refuses path `[12 a 15]`; a carrier of it built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | numbers, root `734c33f3…`, decodes | — |\n| budgets | loop plus 2-cycle under `{:work 1}`: `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`; a valid carrier decoded under `{:work 1}` propagates the same | 3-cycle under `{:search 1}`: `:resource-limit [:search]` | — |\n| key bytes, 4 cells over 2 bodies in `#{#{X Y} #{Z W} #{X Z}}` | label spelling: 4364 bytes at a 128-character descriptor, 920118 at 65536; **rank spelling: 1396 at both**; 13 local runs, 78 `:work`, same root either spelling | — | — |\n| 2000 random value states | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (14 witnessed non-interchangeable by 12 shuffles, 11 not distinguished), **0 invariance failures**, 1783 decode round trips ok, 0 decode failures, 192 not decodable through 6a's own recompute budget (probe limitation); max `:work` 1254 | **2000 numbered, 0 refused**, 0 invariance failures, 1808 decoded, 0 failures, 192 at 6a's budget; max trials 13, max `:work` 1470 | — |\n| 200 frame-bearing states | 192 numbered, 8 refused (5 witnessed, 3 not distinguished), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 767 | 200 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 183 decoded, max trials 5 | — |\n| 100 states with children | 98 numbered, 2 refused (not distinguished), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 916 | 100 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 76 decoded, max trials 4 | — |\n\nHand derivations, verified against the codec above (the digest comes\nfrom `emit-numbered` under the hand-derived numbering; where 6a or the\nreview reached the same numbering independently the digest agrees).\nFixture `:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by the\nkeys of its entries; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\n(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\n`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) ranks before\n`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\ncounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties (`[u0 r]` twice) and is\ndeferred; `'b` meets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-keys the set, `[:n 1]`\nagainst `[:u 0 r]`, and `Y = cc-2`. Review A: keys `[:vec [u0 r] [u0 r]]`\nand `[:vec [u0 r] [u1 r]]` differ at the second cell's index, so `[X X]`\nis entered first: `X = cc-1`, then `Y = cc-2`, `Z = cc-3`. Review B: the\nentries `[:entry [u0 r] [u0 r]]` and `[:entry [u0 r] [u1 r]]` likewise.\nReview C: both inner sets key as `[:set [u0 r] [u1 r]]` (each key run\nresolves its own `{X, Y}` tie by D4 with two one-element components),\nthe outer tie is deferred, the fixpoint changes nothing, the two\ncomponents are isomorphic, and the one listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`:\neither order emits `#{#{1 2} #{3 4}}`. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`: both sets tie\nand defer; components `{X¹, X²}` and `{Y¹, Y²}` (one element per group,\nequal encodings `[:component 1 [u0 r] 2 [u0 r]]`): `X = cc-1`, `Y = cc-2`\nin host order, same rows either way. `[#{X Y} X]`: the set ties, index 1\nmeets `X = cc-1`, the fixpoint splits the set, `Y = cc-2`. **Witness**\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`: F4 above; the key run's residue `[:set [:tie K\nK]]` is the outer set's only key, so the outer element is entered, the\ninner tie deferred, `A = cc-1` at index 1, and the fixpoint gives\n`B = cc-2`. Loop plus 2-cycle: `[L L]` keys as `[u0 r] [u0 r]`, the two\nothers as `[u0 r] [u1 r]`, so `L = cc-1` by key order; `{[A B], [B A]}`\nties, the fixpoint changes nothing, and the component `{[A B], [B A]}`\nholds two elements of one group: D5 refuses; **D6** runs two trials\nover `[:component 1 [:set [A B] [B A]]]`, one entering `[A B]` first\n(`A = u0, B = u1`, then `[B A]` keys `[u1 u0]` and is entered), one the\nmirror image; the two spellings are equal, the first is adopted:\n`A = cc-2`, `B = cc-3`. 2-cycle alone: the same two trials from the empty\nnumbering, `A = cc-1`, `B = cc-2`, and 6a's search reaches the same\nrows. 3-cycle: three trials, each a rotation of the others, equal\nspellings, `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3` in the first trial's order, again\n6a's rows. Path `#{[A B] [B C]}`: two trials, `[A B]` first gives\n`[:set [u0 u1] [u1 u2]]`, `[B C]` first gives `[:set [u2 u0] [u0 u1]]`;\nthe least is adopted. The example state of\n`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` has one cursor cell, first met in\nthe act's environment under `c`, so `cc-1`; a one-cell state has the\nsame rows under both procedures.\n\nPins: none of the round-1 or round-2 pins change (`fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n`a3713a5e…`, `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `4993b5b1…`,\n`536c810b…`, `65ba4530…`, `409548a7…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`,\n`7dc3d5c4…`, `6ea03f9b…`, `b35b8e12…`, the ten-cell root `6e7c7a6b…`),\nwith the rank spelling and with D6 on. New pins: the witness\n`9600fe01…`; under D6 the loop plus 2-cycle `858a891e…` (6a's `55ff168e…`\nstays removed), the 2-cycle `0fe898cc…` and 3-cycle `0b181cd4…` (6a's\nown roots), the path `726a9fbb…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…`.\n\n## 7. Recommendation and cost\n\n**Adopt discovery order with D6** (owner decision: adopt; D6 is this\nnote's recommendation within it). 6b has not started and 6a is\nunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\ntests (§8) plus the amendments of §4. Size, from the prototype and the\nsecond review's count rather than the round-2 guess: the prototype's\nschema-to-procedure region is about 330 nonblank lines, of which about\n30 are D6, the diagnostic mode, tracing and measurement; in place it\nreuses 6a's `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!` and `emit-node`, so expect **250–300 added or replacement\nlines and 135–150 deleted** (`check-label-bytes!`, labels, `ce`,\n`positions`, `classes`, `refine`, `search`), **about 100–160 net**,\nexcluding tests; the second review's independent estimate was 100–195\nnet. Review cost: one implementation round and one review round; the\nreview reads §3 (five lemmas, one induction) and §8's cut list rather\nthan re-deriving the design, and the generator of §9 item 18 runs in CI.\nWhat the owner gains over 6a: a definition that can be stated and proved\n(the n-th cell met, with one bounded tie-break), no growing labels, a\nsearch confined to the residue (two trials for a 2-cycle; 6a's search\nover the seven-cell state exceeded a megabyte of labels, D6 numbers it in\n11 trials), and 6a's own roots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle. Risk: a\ncanonicality bug, mitigated by §9 item 18's generator in CI (it caught\nthe prototype's memo bug), by the decode round trip in that generator,\nand by the proof; and the unproved cost bound, which the `:work` and\n`:search` budgets enforce as 6a's budgets did.\n\n**D5 variant (no search at all).** Same slice minus D6 (about 25 lines),\nplus the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind, its decode translation and the\ndocumented refusal class of F4; refuses 2-cycles and 3-cycles that 6a\nnumbers. Acceptable if the owner wants no search in the codec; the\nrefusal can be replaced by D6 later without changing any id numbered\nunder D1–D4 (§3 consequence 6).\n\n**Keep 6a.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine harness and every\nreviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search over the whole\nstate, labels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte,\nand a definition that is \"whatever the procedure outputs\". Not\nrecommended; the owner has decided against it.\n\n**Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the harness\n(§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\n\n## 8. Replacing 6a: what is deleted, rewritten and kept\n\nAgainst branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\n(line numbers of that file) and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`. An\nimplementation brief can be cut from this list.\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.**\n- `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`): the label-size bound; nothing grows\n  any more.\n- Inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label seeding\n  (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions` (`:285-325`),\n  `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine` (`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`),\n  and the `checked-encode`/`checked-key`/`checked-sort` wrappers\n  (`:245-251`). The `[:child (checked-encode id)]` spelling (`:273-275`)\n  goes with `ce`.\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): the `:label-bytes` key; the `:search`\n  key too in the D5 variant (with D6 it stays and counts trials).\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census →\n  `{}`), now the root run of §2: `descriptors` extended by the two\n  synthetic kinds `:entry` and `:component`; body ranks from the census;\n  `enter`/`unordered`/`fixpoint`/`components`/`component-encoding`/\n  `adopt`/`resolve`/`local-run`/`spell` as in the prototype\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`, sections \"runs\" and\n  \"spelling\"), with `spend!` charged as §2 \"Work\" says; the totality\n  assertion before returning; D5 raising `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` (D5 variant) or D6 charging `:search`.\n  Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection, `mapv`/\n  `into`/`reduce` (not `for`) over sequences that may exceed 32 elements\n  (the prototype's `components` uses `for`; replace), `contains?` for\n  membership, `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`, atoms are fine on all\n  three hosts.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's D5 refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]` (D5 variant only; otherwise unchanged).\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): wrap the `emit` call (which numbers\n  the root and every child) so that only `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` becomes `:yin.k/undecodable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by `root`; a\n  `:resource-limit` propagates (D5 variant only; with D6 the recompute\n  is unchanged apart from the budget keys).\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §2)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, `descriptors`, the census order\n(`:137-156`), `emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!`\n(`:205-209`), `emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`),\n`structural?`, `literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before\n`:570` and after `:575` (the order numbering → `validate-frames`,\n`:576-578`, stays), `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`,\n`rows->frame`, `validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`,\n`:cursor-cells`, `:work` budget keys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept as fixtures and tests.** `task-node`,\n`cursor-cell-body`, `fixture` (`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and the\n`bijection` test, `row-ids-are-jing-default`, `replace-row`,\n`window-sorted-and-unique`, `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`\n(`:143-156`), `alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`),\n`gates-and-graph-before-hashes`, `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`\n(`:206-213`), `schema-and-literal-canonicality`,\n`child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` (`:240-249`),\n`pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), and the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`). Every counterexample of §6 becomes a fixture (items 12–16\nbelow), including the refused ones.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-number-by-the-component-rule`: same two\n  assertions on equal roots and two numbers; the `{:search 1}`\n  assertions become `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block loses the `{:search 2}` assertion and\n  the pin `55ff168e…`; under D6 it pins `858a891e…` with `L = cc-1`\n  (and the renamed task equal); under the D5 variant it asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[A B]`.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-number-by-residue-search` (D6: root `d76d787a…`,\n  11 trials, `{:search 10}` → `:resource-limit [:search]`) or, in the D5\n  variant, `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused` (`:unresolved-cursor-cells`,\n  cells `[a b c d e f g]`).\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-do-not-spend-work-beyond-their-bytes`, same body: a\n  400000-element literal still emits and decodes under the default\n  budget.\n\n## 9. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`,\n    the review counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n    witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` and the two frame fixtures of §6,\n    `canonical-numbering` returns exactly the maps of §6.\n13. `rows-test/review-counterexample-resolves-by-fixpoint`: `'a #{X Y}`,\n    `'b X` numbers `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; the task renamed and with the set\n    reversed gives the same root; the root is pinned `a3713a5e…`.\n14. `rows-test/component-rule`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,\n    `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` number without refusal;\n    element reversal and renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`,\n    `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`; `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}`\n    numbers and is pinned `b35b8e12…`; the witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n    numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`, is pinned `9600fe01…` and decodes.\n16. D6: `rows-test/residue-search`: the 2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the path,\n    the loop plus 2-cycle and the seven-cell state number with the pins\n    of §6 (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`, `726a9fbb…`, `858a891e…`,\n    `d76d787a…`), renaming plus shuffling gives equal roots, every\n    carrier decodes, and the 3-cycle under `{:search 1}` refuses\n    `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:search]`.\n    D5 variant instead: `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the\n    same five states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]` and the component's sorted\n    cursor cells; a carrier of each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to\n    `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed\n    by the root, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose\n    install `'a` holds the 2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path\n    `[12 a 15]` and its `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the\n    `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a3713a5e…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`, `7dc3d5c4…`,\n    `6ea03f9b…`, `9600fe01…` added; `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (one to six\n    cursor cells over two bodies; nested `:vec`, `:set`, `:map` with\n    cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including `[X X]`, sets of\n    sets, under keyed bindings and the result; unique elements and keys\n    under decoded equality) over at least 2000 states, plus at least 200\n    frame-bearing states (cursor cells in an act window, a K-frame\n    window, environments matched to the free environment, and a parked\n    state at a park site) and at least 100 states with install children:\n    every numbered state's carrier decodes and re-emits to its root, and\n    renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every set, map and census\n    gives an equal root id; refusals are counted and reported (0 under\n    D6); the largest `:work` and `:search` charged are reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a task whose census declares a\n    cursor cell the traversal does not reach raises the totality defect\n    (not a refusal) before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n    and `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §8 pass.\n21. `rows-test/work-is-charged-per-byte`: a state whose cursor-cell\n    bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor numbers under the default\n    budget with the same `:work` as under a 128-character descriptor\n    (rank spelling), and `{:work 1}` refuses `:yin.k/unsupported\n    :resource-limit [:work]` at `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at\n    `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` is gone (and `:search` in the D5\n    variant); A1–A9 applied in the variant chosen; kondo clean;\n    `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log shows\n    `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 10. Owner questions\n\n1. **D6 or the D5 variant?** Recommended: D6 (a search confined to one\n   conflicting component, measured at 2–13 trials, 6a's roots on the\n   2-cycle and 3-cycle, no structural refusal class to document). The D5\n   variant is the same slice without about 25 lines, with the\n   `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind and the F4 regression (2-cycles and\n   3-cycles refused) as a documented limitation that D6 can lift later\n   without moving any D1–D4 id.\n2. If the D5 variant: the refusal kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` as\n   proposed, or the orchestrator's `:resource-limit` (recommended\n   against: it is not a budget, and carrier §10.10 says a resource\n   refusal never stands in for a canonicality verdict)?\ncollab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj\ncollab/numbering-probe/deps.edn\ncollab/numbering-probe/run.log\ncollab/numbering-probe/java.args\ncollab/numbering-probe/run.sh\ncollab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\ncollab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\ncollab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc\ncollab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame_test.cljc\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
