{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,470p' collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj\"","aggregated_output":"(ns probe.discovery\n  \"Prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (note D1-D6, round 3 of\n   2026-10-12) over the 6a abstract task representation (yin.vm.ucf.rows).\n   Not repo code; a scratch project under collab/.\n\n   Vocabulary: UCF 7.5.3. A cursor cell is the VM box the carrier serializes;\n   its source id is the lift's local name; the procedure assigns :yin.k/cc-n.\n\n   Shape of the procedure (one algorithm, three uses):\n   - a RUN traverses an item (D1), numbers cursor cells on first meeting (D2),\n     keys every :set / :map element by a speculative local run and defers equal\n     keys to the run's worklist (D1), re-examines the worklist to a fixpoint\n     (D3), groups the surviving tied elements into components by shared\n     unnumbered cursor cells, encodes every component by a local run over its\n     component item and adopts the least encoding's components (D4). A\n     component holding two elements of one group is refused (D5) or, with\n     :residue-search, resolved by trials (D6): one local run per element of\n     the duplicated group with that element entered first, the least\n     spelling adopted.\n   - the root run assigns [:n k], k = 1, 2, ...; a LOCAL run assigns [:u j r]\n     (j from the enclosing run's local counter, r the body rank) and its\n     spelling is the key. A KEY run is speculative: it never refuses, and its\n     residue (ties or conflicts) is spelled [:tie K ...] in the key. A\n     COMPONENT run (and a D6 trial) must be complete, else D5.\n   - :work is charged per position visited or spelled and per literal byte\n     copied into a spelling; :search per D6 trial; exceeding either refuses\n     :yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit, never a canonicality verdict.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]))\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- schema\n\n(def synthetic-table\n  \"Rows that exist only inside the procedure: a :map entry as an ordered pair,\n   and a component as the ordered list of its (group-id item) pairs, where the\n   item is the group's one element or a synthetic :set of its elements.\"\n  {:entry [:V :V] :component [:pairs :D :V]})\n\n(defn descriptors [kind slots]\n  (let [schema (or (get rows/row-table kind) (get synthetic-table kind))]\n    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\n          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n          :else schema)))\n\n(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\n\n(defn bytes-key [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\n\n(defn compare-bytes [a b]\n  (loop [i 0]\n    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n          (= i (count b)) 1\n          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n\n(defn by-key-bytes [f xs] (sort #(compare-bytes (f %1) (f %2)) xs))\n\n(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table})\n\n(defn elem-item\n  \"The item an element is entered and keyed as: a :set element is its V; a\n   :map entry [k v] is the synthetic ordered row [:entry k v], so that the\n   alias pattern across key and value is part of the key.\"\n  [kind e]\n  (if (= :map kind) [:row {:kind :entry :slots (vec e)}] e))\n\n(defn child-task? [ctx n] (and (= :task (:kind n)) (not (identical? n (:task ctx)))))\n\n;; --------------------------------------------------------------- budgets\n\n(def default-budget\n  \"6a's budget without :label-bytes; :search now counts D6 trials.\"\n  (dissoc rows/default-budget :label-bytes))\n\n(defn- spend! [ctx category amount]\n  (let [n (get (swap! (:counter ctx) update category (fnil + 0) amount) category)]\n    (when (> n (get (:budget ctx) category))\n      (throw (ex-info \"resource limit\"\n                      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :resource-limit :yin.k/path [category]})))))\n\n(defn- literal-bytes [x] (count (cbor/encode x)))\n\n(defn item-cells\n  \"Cursor cells occurring in slot (d x); census and install children excluded.\n   Memoized per procedure on the slot value.\"\n  [ctx d x]\n  (or (get @(:cells-memo ctx) [d x])\n      (let [acc (atom #{})]\n        (letfn [(slot [d x]\n                  (cond (ref? d) (node x)\n                        (refs? d) (run! node x)\n                        (= :C d) (swap! acc conj x)\n                        (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\n                                         :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second x))\n                                         :row (node (second x))\n                                         nil))\n                        :else nil))\n                (node [n]\n                  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n                    (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells kind) (child-task? ctx n))\n                      (dorun (map slot (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n          (slot d x)\n          (swap! (:cells-memo ctx) assoc [d x] @acc)\n          @acc))))\n\n(defn body-label [body]\n  (let [[stream position] (:slots body)]\n    [:body (into [:stream] (:slots stream)) position]))\n\n(defn body-ranks\n  \"Cursor cell -> rank of its body label among the census's distinct body\n   labels in bytewise order. Rank order equals label order, so spelling the\n   rank instead of the label changes no comparison and no numbering; it keeps\n   key bytes independent of the descriptor size.\"\n  [bodies]\n  (let [labels (into {} (map (fn [[c b]] [c (body-label b)])) bodies)\n        ranks (into {} (map-indexed (fn [i l] [l i]))\n                    (by-key-bytes bytes-key (distinct (vals labels))))]\n    (into {} (map (fn [[c l]] [c (get ranks l)])) labels)))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ runs\n\n(def ^:dynamic *trace* false)\n\n(defn- new-run [ctx num tag next first-item]\n  {:ctx ctx :num (atom num) :tag tag :next (atom next) :first (atom first-item)\n   :groups (atom []) :gid (atom 0) :refused (atom nil)})\n\n(defn- meet!\n  \"D2. A cursor cell met for the first time receives the run's next number.\"\n  [run c]\n  (when-not (contains? @(:num run) c)\n    (let [k @(:next run)]\n      (swap! (:num run) assoc c [(:tag run) k])\n      (swap! (:next run) inc)\n      true)))\n\n(declare enter-node! enter-slot! unordered! local-run resolve! spell-slot spell-node)\n\n(defn- enter-slot! [run d x path]\n  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n  (cond (ref? d) (enter-node! run x path)\n        (refs? d) (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i n] (enter-node! run n (conj path i))) x))\n        (= :C d) (meet! run x)\n        (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\n                         :cursor-cell (meet! run (second x))\n                         :row (enter-node! run (second x) path)\n                         nil))\n        :else nil))\n\n(defn- enter-node!\n  \"D1. Canonical traversal: table order for slots; ordered repeated slots in\n   order; keyed structural rows in the Jing byte order of the key; :window in\n   ascending register order; installs and module stores by name; :set and\n   :map by local keys with ties deferred; census and children not entered.\"\n  [run n path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n    (cond\n      (= :cursor-cells kind) nil\n      (child-task? ctx n) nil\n      (= :task kind)\n      (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\n        (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n          (doseq [child (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first (:slots %))) x)]\n            (enter-node! run child (conj path i (first (:slots child)))))\n          (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))))\n      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n      (doseq [[k v] (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first %)) (partition 2 slots))]\n        (enter-slot! run (second ds) v (conj path k)))\n      (= :window kind)\n      (doseq [[r v] (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))] (enter-slot! run :V v (conj path r)))\n      (= :set kind) (unordered! run :set (vec slots) path)\n      (= :map kind) (unordered! run :map (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)) path)\n      :else (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n\n(defn- local-key [run item]\n  (let [r (local-run run item nil)] [(bytes-key (:spell r)) (:spell r)]))\n\n(defn- unordered!\n  \"D1 for an unordered container: key every element by a speculative local\n   run under the numbering at this moment; enter singletons in key order;\n   defer equal runs as one tie group (deferral order = canonical order). A D6\n   trial run enters its forced element first, when its container is keyed.\"\n  [run kind elems path]\n  (let [f @(:first run)\n        forced (when f (first (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when (= f (elem-item kind e)) i)) elems)))]\n    (when forced\n      (reset! (:first run) nil)\n      (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind (nth elems forced)) (conj path forced)))\n    (let [keyed (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when (not= i forced) [(local-key run (elem-item kind e)) e i])) elems))\n          groups (partition-by (comp first first) (sort #(compare-bytes (first (first %1)) (first (first %2))) keyed))]\n      (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n        (println \"  [sort]\" path \"num\" @(:num run))\n        (doseq [g groups] (println \"    \" (if (= 1 (count g)) \"enter\" \"DEFER\") (mapv (fn [[[_ sp] e _]] [e sp]) g))))\n      (doseq [g groups]\n        (if (= 1 (count g))\n          (let [[_ e i] (first g)] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path i)))\n          (swap! (:groups run) conj {:id (swap! (:gid run) inc) :kind kind :path path\n                                     :key (second (first (first g))) :elems (mapv second g)}))))))\n\n(defn- fixpoint!\n  \"D3. Re-examine every deferred group in deferral order under the current\n   numbering; repeat while a round numbers a cursor cell and groups remain.\"\n  [run]\n  (loop []\n    (let [before (count @(:num run)) groups @(:groups run)]\n      (reset! (:groups run) [])\n      (doseq [{:keys [kind elems path]} groups] (unordered! run kind elems path))\n      (when (and (seq @(:groups run)) (> (count @(:num run)) before)) (recur)))))\n\n(defn- components\n  \"D4. Tied elements of all surviving groups, linked by shared unnumbered\n   cursor cells (union-find). A component is :conflict when it holds two\n   elements of one group; :dup-group is the least such group.\"\n  [run]\n  (let [num @(:num run) ctx (:ctx run)\n        by-id (into {} (map (juxt :id identity)) @(:groups run))\n        elems (vec (for [g @(:groups run) [i e] (map-indexed vector (:elems g))]\n                     {:group (:id g) :kind (:kind g) :elem e :idx i\n                      :cells (set (remove #(contains? num %) (item-cells ctx :V (elem-item (:kind g) e))))}))\n        parent (atom (vec (range (count elems))))\n        find (fn find [i] (let [p (@parent i)] (if (= p i) i (let [r (find p)] (swap! parent assoc i r) r))))\n        union (fn [i j] (let [ri (find i) rj (find j)] (when (not= ri rj) (swap! parent assoc ri rj))))\n        by-cell (reduce (fn [m [i el]] (reduce (fn [m c] (update m c (fnil conj []) i)) m (:cells el)))\n                        {} (map-indexed vector elems))]\n    (doseq [[_ is] by-cell] (doseq [j (rest is)] (union (first is) j)))\n    (->> (group-by find (range (count elems)))\n         (sort-by first)\n         (mapv (fn [[_ is]]\n                 (let [members (vec (sort-by (juxt :group :idx) (map elems is)))\n                       dup (first (sort (keep (fn [[g c]] (when (> c 1) g)) (frequencies (map :group members)))))]\n                   {:members members\n                    :conflict (some? dup)\n                    :dup-group dup\n                    :path (:path (get by-id (or dup (:group (first members)))))\n                    :cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat :cells members))))}))))))\n\n(defn- component-item\n  \"[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …] in group order; iᵢ is the group's one element\n   in the component, or a synthetic :set of its elements when there are\n   several (the D6 case).\"\n  [comp]\n  [:row {:kind :component\n         :slots (vec (mapcat (fn [ms]\n                               [(:group (first ms))\n                                (if (= 1 (count ms))\n                                  (elem-item (:kind (first ms)) (:elem (first ms)))\n                                  [:row {:kind :set :slots (mapv #(elem-item (:kind %) (:elem %)) ms)}])])\n                             (partition-by :group (:members comp))))}])\n\n(defn- component-encoding\n  \"D4 for a certified component: a local run over its item, required to be\n   complete. D5 for a conflicting component: refuse. D6 (:residue-search): one\n   trial per element of the duplicated group, that element entered first; the\n   least complete spelling is the encoding. :permissive (diagnostic only):\n   a single trial with the host-first element.\"\n  [run comp]\n  (let [ctx (:ctx run) item (component-item comp)]\n    (cond\n      (not (:conflict comp)) (local-run run item nil)\n      (:permissive ctx)\n      (let [m (first (filter #(= (:dup-group comp) (:group %)) (:members comp)))]\n        (local-run run item (elem-item (:kind m) (:elem m))))\n      (:residue-search ctx)\n      (let [es (filter #(= (:dup-group comp) (:group %)) (:members comp))\n            trials (mapv (fn [m] (spend! ctx :search 1) (local-run run item (elem-item (:kind m) (:elem m)))) es)]\n        (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n          (doseq [t trials] (println \"  [trial]\" (:complete? t) (:spell t))))\n        (or (first (remove :complete? trials))\n            (first (sort-by #(bytes-key (:spell %)) compare-bytes trials))))\n      :else {:complete? false\n             :refused {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells :path (:path comp) :cursor-cells (:cells comp)}})))\n\n(defn- adopt!\n  \"D4. Enter a component by adopting its local run: its fresh cursor cells\n   receive the enclosing run's next numbers in local-index order.\"\n  [run comp]\n  (let [fresh (->> (:num (:ce comp))\n                   (remove (fn [[c _]] (contains? @(:num run) c)))\n                   (sort-by (comp second val)))]\n    (doseq [[c _] fresh] (meet! run c))))\n\n(defn- remove-elements! [run comps]\n  (let [gone (set (for [c comps m (:members c)] [(:group m) (:idx m)]))]\n    (swap! (:groups run)\n           (fn [gs] (vec (keep (fn [g]\n                                 (let [es (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when-not (gone [(:id g) i]) e)) (:elems g)))]\n                                   (when (seq es) (assoc g :elems es))))\n                               gs))))))\n\n(defn- resolve!\n  \"D3-D6 to completion. Leaves :refused set (D5) with the worklist as of the\n   last fixpoint, or the worklist empty.\"\n  [run]\n  (loop []\n    (fixpoint! run)\n    (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n      (println \"  [resolve] num\" @(:num run) \"groups\" (mapv (fn [g] [(:id g) (:path g) (mapv (fn [e] (if (= :cursor-cell (first e)) (second e) e)) (:elems g))]) @(:groups run))))\n    (when (seq @(:groups run))\n      (let [comps (mapv #(assoc % :ce (component-encoding run %)) (components run))\n            bad (seq (remove #(:complete? (:ce %)) comps))]\n        (if bad\n          ;; The verdict is invariant; the diagnostic names every conflicting\n          ;; component's cells (sorted) and the least conflicting group's path,\n          ;; so it does not depend on host order either.\n          (reset! (:refused run) {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n                                  :path (:path (:refused (:ce (first (sort-by #(or (:dup-group %) Long/MAX_VALUE) bad)))))\n                                  :cursor-cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat #(:cursor-cells (:refused (:ce %))) bad))))})\n          (let [sorted (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %1))) (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %2)))) comps)\n                least (bytes-key (:spell (:ce (first sorted))))\n                least-run (take-while #(= least (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %)))) sorted)]\n            (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n              (doseq [c sorted] (println \"  [component]\" (mapv (fn [m] [(:group m) (:cells m)]) (:members c)) \"CE\" (:spell (:ce c))))\n              (println \"  [adopt]\" (count least-run)))\n            (doseq [c least-run] (adopt! run c))\n            (remove-elements! run least-run)\n            (recur)))))))\n\n(defn- local-run\n  \"A nested run on one item from the enclosing run's numbering, tag :u. Its\n   result is a key: the spelling under its final numbering, surviving ties\n   spelled [:tie K ...]. `first-item`, when given (D6 trial), is entered\n   before its siblings. Memoized on (item, numbering restricted to the item's\n   cursor cells, local counter, first-item).\"\n  [run item first-item]\n  (let [ctx (:ctx run)\n        cells (item-cells ctx :V item)\n        num @(:num run)\n        start (if (= :u (:tag run)) @(:next run) 0)\n        mk [item (select-keys num cells) start first-item]]\n    (or (get @(:memo ctx) mk)\n        (let [sub (new-run ctx num :u start first-item)]\n          (enter-slot! sub :V item [])\n          (resolve! sub)\n          (let [spell (spell-slot sub :V item [])\n                r {:spell spell\n                   ;; Restricted to the item's own cursor cells: the result is a\n                   ;; function of the memo key, never of the enclosing context.\n                   :num (select-keys @(:num sub) cells)\n                   :complete? (and (nil? @(:refused sub)) (empty? @(:groups sub)))\n                   :refused @(:refused sub)}]\n            (swap! (:counter ctx) update :key-bytes (fnil + 0) (count (bytes-key spell)))\n            (swap! (:counter ctx) update :local-runs (fnil + 0) 1)\n            (swap! (:memo ctx) assoc mk r)\n            r)))))\n\n;; -------------------------------------------------------------- spelling\n\n(defn- spell-cell [run c]\n  (let [[t k] (get @(:num run) c) ctx (:ctx run)]\n    (case t\n      :n [:cursor-cell [:n k]]\n      :u [:cursor-cell [:u k (if (= :label (:spell-body ctx))\n                               (body-label (get (:bodies ctx) c))\n                               (get (:ranks ctx) c))]]\n      (throw (ex-info \"spelling an unnumbered cursor cell\" {:cursor-cell c})))))\n\n(defn- spell-slot [run d x path]\n  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n  (cond (ref? d) (spell-node run x path)\n        (refs? d) (vec (map-indexed (fn [i n] (spell-node run n (conj path i))) x))\n        (= :C d) (spell-cell run x)\n        (v? d) (if (nil? x) x\n                   (case (first x)\n                     :cursor-cell (spell-cell run (second x))\n                     :row [:row (spell-node run (second x) path)]\n                     (do (spend! (:ctx run) :work (literal-bytes (second x))) x)))\n        :else (do (spend! (:ctx run) :work (literal-bytes x)) x)))\n\n(defn- spell-node\n  \"The content encoding CE of a row under the run's final numbering.\"\n  [run n path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n    (cond\n      (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\n      (child-task? ctx n) [:child]\n      (= :task kind)\n      (into [kind]\n            (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]]\n                           (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n                             (vec (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (nth % 1))\n                                                (map #(spell-node run % (conj path i (first (:slots %)))) x)))\n                             (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))))\n                         (map vector ds slots)))\n      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n      (into [kind] (mapcat identity (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first %))\n                                                  (map (fn [[k v]] [k (spell-slot run (second ds) v (conj path k))])\n                                                       (partition 2 slots)))))\n      (= :window kind)\n      (into [kind] (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [r (spell-slot run :V v (conj path r))]) (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n      (contains? #{:set :map} kind)\n      (let [elems (if (= :set kind) (vec slots) (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)))\n            tied (filter #(= path (:path %)) @(:groups run))\n            tied-elems (set (mapcat :elems tied))\n            plain (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when-not (tied-elems e) (spell-slot run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path i)))) elems)\n            ties (map #(into [:tie] (repeat (count (:elems %)) (:key %))) tied)]\n        (into [kind] (by-key-bytes bytes-key (concat plain ties))))\n      :else (into [kind] (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------- procedure\n\n(def ^:dynamic *numbering-opts*\n  \"Options for every run of the procedure in scope (root and install children):\n   :residue-search (D6), :permissive (diagnostic), :spell-body :label (the\n   round-2 spelling, for the byte measurement), :budget.\"\n  {})\n\n(defn discovery-numbering\n  \"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n} :used {...}} or {:refused {...}}.\n   Throws :resource-limit when the budget is exceeded, and a defect (never a\n   refusal) when the numbering is not total.\"\n  ([task] (discovery-numbering task *numbering-opts*))\n  ([task opts]\n   (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\n         bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\n         ctx {:task task\n              :bodies bodies\n              :ranks (body-ranks bodies)\n              :memo (atom {}) :cells-memo (atom {})\n              :budget (merge default-budget (:budget opts))\n              :counter (atom {})\n              :permissive (:permissive opts)\n              :residue-search (:residue-search opts)\n              :spell-body (:spell-body opts)}\n         run (new-run ctx {} :n 1 nil)]\n     (enter-node! run task [])\n     (resolve! run)\n     (if-let [r @(:refused run)]\n       {:refused r :used @(:counter ctx)}\n       (let [numbered (set (keys @(:num run))) declared (set (keys bodies))]\n         (when (seq @(:groups run)) (throw (ex-info \"residue after resolve\" {})))\n         (when (not= numbered declared)\n           (throw (ex-info \"numbering is not total\" {:missing (vec (remove numbered declared))\n                                                      :extra (vec (remove declared numbered))})))\n         {:numbering (into {} (map (fn [[c [_ k]]] [c (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"cc-\" k))])) @(:num run))\n          :used @(:counter ctx)})))))\n\n;; --------------------------------------------------------------- emission\n;; 6a's emit-node with install children numbered by discovery order and a\n;; row path threaded for the child refusal.\n\n(def ^:private ordered-slots @#'rows/ordered-slots)\n(def ^:private check-literal-markers! @#'rows/check-literal-markers!)\n(def ^:private byte-compare @#'rows/byte-compare)\n\n(declare emit-node*)\n\n(defn- refuse-unresolved! [r path]\n  (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n                   :yin.k/path (into path (:path r)) :yin.k/cursor-cells (:cursor-cells r)})))\n\n(defn- emit-slot* [d x numbering rows path]\n  (when-not (or (ref? d) (refs? d) (= :C d))\n    (check-literal-markers! (if (v? d) (when (= :lit (first x)) (second x)) x)))\n  (cond\n    (ref? d) (emit-node* x numbering rows path)\n    (refs? d) (reduce (fn [[out rs] [j n]]\n                        (let [p (conj path (if (= :install (:kind n)) (first (:slots n)) j))\n                              [id rs'] (emit-node* n numbering rs p)]\n                          [(conj out id) rs']))\n                      [[] rows] (map-indexed vector x))\n    (= d :C) [(get numbering x x) rows]\n    (v? d) (case (first x)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '470,720p' collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj; sed -n '120,430p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md; cat collab/numbering-probe/run.sh; cat collab/numbering-probe/java.args\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '470,720p' collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj; sed -n '120,430p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md; cat collab/numbering-probe/run.sh; cat collab/numbering-probe/java.args\"","aggregated_output":"    (v? d) (case (first x)\n             :row (let [[id rs] (emit-node* (second x) numbering rows path)] [[:row id] rs])\n             :cursor-cell [[:cursor-cell (get numbering (second x) (second x))] rows]\n             [x rows])\n    :else [x rows]))\n\n(defn- emit-node* [n numbering rows path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n\n        [slots rows] (reduce (fn [[out rs] [i d x]]\n                               (if (and (ref? d) (= :task (:kind x)))\n                                 ;; An install child: its own scope, numbered by the same procedure.\n                                 (let [r (discovery-numbering x)\n                                       _ (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) path))\n                                       [id child-rows] (emit-node* x (:numbering r) {} [])]\n                                   [(conj out id) (merge rs child-rows)])\n                                 (let [[x' rs'] (emit-slot* d x numbering rs (conj path i))] [(conj out x') rs'])))\n                             [[] rows] (map vector (range) (descriptors kind slots) slots))\n        slots (if (= kind :task)\n                (-> slots\n                    (update 7 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %)))\n                    (update 12 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %))))\n                (ordered-slots kind slots))\n        body (into [kind] slots) id (jing/segment-key body)]\n    [id (assoc rows id (into [id] body))]))\n\n(defn emit*\n  \"Lift side: number by discovery order, emit; refuses :yin.k/non-portable\n   :unresolved-cursor-cells (D5) for the root or for any install child (path\n   [12 <name> …]); a budget refusal propagates as itself.\"\n  [task]\n  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\n    (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) []))\n    (let [[id rows] (emit-node* task (:numbering r) {} [])]\n      {:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root id :yin.k/rows (vec (vals rows))})))\n\n(defn decode*\n  \"Receiver side: 6a's decode* with its own numbering check off (it still runs\n   6a's recompute, under 6a's budget), then the whole discovery recomputation\n   (root and install children) under one translation: only a lift-side\n   :yin.k/non-portable of kind :unresolved-cursor-cells becomes\n   :yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells with the path prefixed by the\n   carried root id; anything else (a resource refusal) propagates as itself.\n   In the real codec this check sits at carrier step 6, before\n   validate-frames; here it runs after 6a's decode* returns.\"\n  [wire]\n  (let [task (@#'rows/decode* wire (assoc rows/default-budget :check-numbering false))\n        root (:yin.k/root wire)\n        id (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n                (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n                  (let [d (ex-data e)]\n                    (if (and (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status d)) (= :unresolved-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind d)))\n                      (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n                                      (assoc d :yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/path (into [root] (:yin.k/path d)))))\n                      (throw e)))))]\n    (when (not= id root)\n      (throw (ex-info \"noncanonical\" {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :noncanonical-cursor-cells\n                                      :yin.k/path [root]})))\n    task))\n\n(defn error [f]\n  (try (f) nil (catch Throwable e (or (ex-data e) {:throwable (str e)}))))\n\n(defn do-root\n  \"Root id, {:refused …} for D5, or {:resource-limit path} for a budget.\"\n  [task]\n  (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n       (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n         (let [d (ex-data e)]\n           (case (:yin.k/kind d)\n             :unresolved-cursor-cells {:refused d}\n             :resource-limit {:resource-limit (:yin.k/path d)}\n             (throw e))))))\n\n(defn safe-6a-root [task]\n  (try (:yin.k/root (rows/emit task))\n       (catch Throwable e {:refused (select-keys (ex-data e) [:yin.k/kind :yin.k/path])})))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ generators\n\n(defn rename-and-shuffle\n  \"Rename every cursor cell by `bij` and shuffle every :set, :map and the\n   census in listed order (shuffle is rebound by callers for determinism).\"\n  [n renaming]\n  (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v)\n                       :cursor-cell [:cursor-cell (bij (second v))]\n                       :row [:row (go (second v))]\n                       v))\n          (bij [x] (get renaming x x))\n          (go [n]\n            (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n              (case kind\n                :set (rows/node :set (vec (shuffle (mapv value slots))))\n                :map (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (shuffle (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(value k) (value v)]) (partition 2 slots))))))\n                :cursor-cells (rows/node :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat (fn [[c b]] [(bij c) (go b)]) (shuffle (mapv vec (partition 2 slots))))))\n                (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\n                                        (cond (ref? d) (go x)\n                                              (refs? d) (mapv go x)\n                                              (= :C d) (bij x)\n                                              (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\n                                              :else x))\n                                      (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n    (go n)))\n\n(defn all-census-cells\n  \"Source ids of every census in the tree (root and install children).\"\n  [task]\n  (let [acc (atom [])]\n    (letfn [(node [n] (when (map? n)\n                        (when (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (swap! acc into (take-nth 2 (:slots n))))\n                        (doseq [[d x] (map vector (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n))]\n                          (cond (ref? d) (node x) (refs? d) (run! node x)\n                                (v? d) (when (and x (= :row (first x))) (node (second x)))))))]\n      (node task) (vec @acc))))\n\n(defn seeded-shuffle [^java.util.Random r]\n  (fn [xs] (let [a (java.util.ArrayList. ^java.util.Collection (vec xs))]\n             (java.util.Collections/shuffle a r)\n             (vec a))))\n\n(defn decoded\n  \"Portable decoded equality of an abstract value (check-abstract!'s rule):\n   sets as sets, maps as maps, cursor cells by identity.\"\n  [v]\n  (case (first v)\n    :lit [:lit (second v)]\n    :cursor-cell [:identity (second v)]\n    :row (let [n (second v) ss (:slots n)]\n           (case (:kind n)\n             :vec [:seq (mapv decoded ss)]\n             :set [:set (set (map decoded ss))]\n             :map [:map (into {} (map (fn [[k x]] [(decoded k) (decoded x)])) (partition 2 ss))]\n             [:row n]))))\n\n(defn distinct-decoded [vs] (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [e] [(decoded e) e])) vs))))\n\n(defn gen-value\n  \"Random V over `cells`, depth-bounded. Shapes: literals, cursor cells,\n   vectors, sets, maps (cursor cells as keys too), sets of cursor-cell pairs\n   (edges, aliases such as [X X] included), sets of sets.\"\n  [^java.util.Random r cells depth]\n  (let [pick (fn [xs] (nth xs (.nextInt r (count xs))))\n        cc (fn [] [:cursor-cell (pick cells)])\n        k (.nextInt r (if (pos? depth) 9 3))]\n    (case k\n      0 [:lit (pick [1 2 :k \"s\" nil])]\n      1 (cc)\n      2 (cc)\n      3 (let [elems (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :vec elems)] [:lit (mapv second elems)]))\n      4 (let [elems (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 4)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :set elems)] [:lit (set (map second elems))]))\n      5 (let [pairs (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [p] [(decoded (first p)) p]))\n                                     (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector (gen-value r cells (dec depth)) (gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))))]\n          (if (some (fn [[a b]] (or (not= :lit (first a)) (not= :lit (first b)))) pairs)\n            [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity pairs)))]\n            [:lit (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(second a) (second b)])) pairs)]))\n      (6 7) (let [edges (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector :row (rows/node :vec [(cc) (cc)]))))]\n              [:row (rows/node :set edges)])\n      8 (let [inner (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 2))\n                                                  #(let [es (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) cc))]\n                                                     [:row (rows/node :set es)])))]\n          [:row (rows/node :set inner)]))))\n\n(defn reached [v]\n  (let [acc (atom #{})]\n    (letfn [(w [v] (case (first v)\n                     :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second v))\n                     :row (doseq [s (:slots (second v))] (w s))\n                     nil))]\n      (w v) @acc)))\n\n(defn gen-task\n  \"A :halted task: 1-6 cursor cells over two bodies (ties common), 1-3 store\n   bindings and the result slot hold random values.\"\n  [seed]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n        k (inc (.nextInt r 6))\n        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"c\" %)) (range k))\n        bodies (mapv (fn [_] (rt/cursor-cell-body :s (if (zero? (.nextInt r 3)) 1 0))) cells)\n        names (vec (take (inc (.nextInt r 3)) '[a b c]))\n        vals (mapv (fn [_] (gen-value r cells 3)) names)\n        result (gen-value r cells 2)\n        used (reduce into (reached result) (map reached vals))\n        census (keep (fn [[c b]] (when (used c) [c b])) (map vector cells bodies))]\n    (if (empty? used)\n      (recur (inc seed))\n      (let [t (rt/task-node census result)]\n        (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store (vec (mapcat (fn [n v] [n v]) names vals)))))))))\n\n(defn worked-act []\n  (first (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame)))))\n\n(def park-vector [[:closure 0 [] 2] [:halt 0] [:park 0] [:return 0]])\n(def park-address (jing/segment-key park-vector))\n\n(defn frame-task\n  \"A blocked task carrying the worked code plus a park segment. The wait's\n   act holds `wait-values` in its window register 1 and in one K frame's\n   window register 1; `env-value`, when given, is bound to e0 in the free\n   environment and in every environment; the parked state (P 7) is an act\n   at the park site whose K frame holds `parked-value` in register 1.\n   Decodes under 6a's validate-frames.\"\n  [census wait-values env-value parked-value]\n  (let [base (rt/task-node (conj (vec census) [:test/cursor (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]) nil)\n        [seg pc env window k rd] (:slots (worked-act))\n        [_ store-of] (:slots env)\n        bindings (rows/node :bindings (if env-value ['e0 env-value] []))\n        env (rows/node :env [bindings store-of])\n        kframe (fn [v] (rows/node :kframe [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 v]) rd]))\n        [w1 w2] wait-values\n        wait-act (rows/node :act [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 w1]) (rows/node :k (if w2 [(kframe w2)] [])) rd])\n        pending (second (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame))))\n        parked (rows/node :act [park-address 3 env (rows/node :window []) (rows/node :k [(kframe parked-value)]) 0])]\n    (rows/node :task (-> (:slots base) (assoc 2 :blocked) (assoc 5 bindings)\n                         (assoc 11 (merge ft/worked-code {park-address park-vector}))\n                         (assoc 8 (rows/node :parked-table (if parked-value [7 parked] [])))\n                         (assoc 13 [(rows/node :wait [wait-act pending])]) (assoc 16 {:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork})))))\n\n(defn gen-frame-task [seed]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n        k (inc (.nextInt r 4))\n        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"f\" %)) (range k))\n        body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        pick (fn [] (gen-value r cells 2))\n        wait-values (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 2)) pick))\n        env-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n        parked-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n        used (reduce into #{} (map reached (remove nil? (concat wait-values [env-value parked-value]))))]\n    (if (empty? used)\n      (recur (inc seed))\n      (frame-task (mapv #(vector % body) (filter used cells)) wait-values env-value parked-value))))\n\n(defn gen-child-task\n  \"A parent (seed) with two install children: one random child task and the\n   review counterexample as a child; the parent's own values stay random.\"\n  [seed]\n  (let [parent (gen-task seed)\n        child (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (gen-task (+ 100000 seed))) 3 :child))]\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12 [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} child])\n                                                (rows/node :install ['b :running nil {} child])]))))\n\n(defn fixture-review-counterexample\n  \"'a bound to #{X Y} with equal bodies, 'b bound to X.\"\n  []\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        t (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:lit nil])]\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store ['a [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]\n                                                             'b [:cursor-cell :x]])))))\n\n(defn with-child\n  \"`parent` with one install 'a holding `child` (slot 3 set to :child).\"\nprovisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so\na key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length\nof a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\").\n\n**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's\nfixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur\nonly inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an\nunnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\n**component**. Two cases:\n- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\n  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in\n  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,\n  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by\n  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\n  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).\n  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and\n  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\n- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then\n  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be\n  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component\n  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a\n  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`\n  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.\n  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`\n  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is\n  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets\n  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)\n  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the\n  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).\n  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is\n  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.\n  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the\n  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which\n  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected\n  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*\n\n  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more\n  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,\n  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in\n  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The\n  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed\n  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the\n  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,\n  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell\n  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and\n  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under\n  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts\n  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector\n  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole\n  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in\n  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no\n  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census\n  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural\n  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register\n  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a\n  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on\n  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of\n  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\n  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).\n  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,\n  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:\n  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over\n  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a\n  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in\n  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle\n  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.\n  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6\n  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.\n\n**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\nrefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\nof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\ncensus) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2\n\"Work\".\n\n**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\ncursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\nmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\nsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\nframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\nstate) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself\nrenamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance\nfailures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6\nrefuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default\n200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1\nand 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);\nthe second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes\nof one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label\nspelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character\ndescriptor.\n\n**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n\n## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n\nInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\ncells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\ntask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\nrefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\nrule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n\n**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\ntraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\nnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\nrun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\nenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the\ncell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,\nso no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,\nand the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor\ncell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else\nchanges from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`\nexpanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\n`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's\n`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install\nentry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides\nan order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are\nspelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register\norder; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its\nelements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,\nwhere a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as\n`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are\nbytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free.\n\n**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\nrow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\nkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\nspecifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is\nnot entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a\n**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering\nat the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that\none numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\nequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\norder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\ncontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\nan entry's key before its value.\n\n**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\ncell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\ncounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\nlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\nenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\nno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\ncontents table.\n\n**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\nre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\nthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\nre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\ncell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\nit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)\njoins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is\none worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred\ngroups from every depth.\n\n**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\nlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\ncell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\nfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\nso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component\nby a component run over the synthetic ordered row\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral\nsequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that\ngroup, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a\nsynthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be\ncomplete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it\nreaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the\ncomponents by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:\neach of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,\nin that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's\nnumbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,\nlemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).\nRemove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group\nwhose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\ncarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\nhost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n\n**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when\nit holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the\nroot run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component\nrun of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed\n`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered\ncursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the\ndiagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run\nnever refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing\nrefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is\na resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\n**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A\nconflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting\ngroup of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each\n`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`\nrow in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before\nits siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local\nindices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to\ncompletion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The\ncomponent's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the\ncomponent adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are\nisomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no\nstructural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the\nresource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not\ndefined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts\ntrials; without it, the key is deleted.\n\n**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\nBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\nbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\ndefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\nrefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\n\n**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\nlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\nrestricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and\n(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and\nmust return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the\nprototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of\nanother element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo\nholds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no\nunaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is\nclaimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound.\n\n**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\nthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\non mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\n(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*\n(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\n(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`\nkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the\ncarried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);\na resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\nitself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\nalready checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an\ninstall child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path\n`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the\nprototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The\nvalidator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before\n`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the\ntranslation has nothing to translate and is omitted.\n\n**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the\nround-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream\ndescriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and\nthat memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.\nThe procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:\nthe `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,\nevery local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per\nbyte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the\ncopy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the\nstructural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the\ncarried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the\nresource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`\nwas. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,\nso the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the\ncarried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:\na key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of\nthe number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key\nbytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound\nwithout memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and\n`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,\n`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor\ncells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than\nthe analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`\nunits, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default\n200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The\n`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels.\n\n## 3. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\n**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\nother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\norder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\n(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\nThe proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of\nunnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):\na key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more\nunnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested\nunordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so\nthe lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns.\n\n**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\nof a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks\n(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and\nnumbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested\ncontainers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,\n(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,\ncovered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a\nconflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is\na function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under\ncorresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order\nof a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism.\n\n**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\nconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\n\n**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\nof one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let\n`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise\ndisjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an\n#!/bin/sh\n# Run the discovery-order numbering prototype from the probe directory on\n# the launcher's cached classpath (java.