]\n   365\t  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n   366\t  (cond (ref? d) (spell-node run x path)\n   367\t        (refs? d) (vec (map-indexed (fn [i n] (spell-node run n (conj path i))) x))\n   368\t        (= :C d) (spell-cell run x)\n   369\t        (v? d) (if (nil? x) x\n   370\t                   (case (first x)\n   409\t  \"Options for every run of the procedure in scope (root and install children):\n   410\t   :residue-search (D6), :permissive (diagnostic), :spell-body :label (the\n   411\t   round-2 spelling, for the byte measurement), :budget.\"\n   412\t  {})\n   413\t\n   414\t(defn discovery-numbering\n   415\t  \"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n} :used {...}} or {:refused {...}}.\n   416\t   Throws :resource-limit when the budget is exceeded, and a defect (never a\n   417\t   refusal) when the numbering is not total.\"\n   418\t  ([task] (discovery-numbering task *numbering-opts*))\n   419\t  ([task opts]\n   420\t   (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\n   421\t         bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\n   422\t         ctx {:task task\n   423\t              :bodies bodies\n   424\t              :ranks (body-ranks bodies)\n   425\t              :memo (atom {}) :cells-memo (atom {})\n   426\t              :budget (merge default-budget (:budget opts))\n   427\t              :counter (atom {})\n   428\t              :permissive (:permissive opts)\n   429\t              :residue-search (:residue-search opts)\n   430\t              :spell-body (:spell-body opts)}\n   431\t         run (new-run ctx {} :n 1 nil)]\n   432\t     (enter-node! run task [])\n   433\t     (resolve! run)\n   434\t     (if-let [r @(:refused run)]\n   435\t       {:refused r :used @(:counter ctx)}\n   436\t       (let [numbered (set (keys @(:num run))) declared (set (keys bodies))]\n   437\t         (when (seq @(:groups run)) (throw (ex-info \"residue after resolve\" {})))\n   438\t         (when (not= numbered declared)\n   439\t           (throw (ex-info \"numbering is not total\" {:missing (vec (remove numbered declared))\n   440\t                                                      :extra (vec (remove declared numbered))})))\n   441\t         {:numbering (into {} (map (fn [[c [_ k]]] [c (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"cc-\" k))])) @(:num run))\n   442\t          :used @(:counter ctx)})))))\n   443\t\n   444\t;; --------------------------------------------------------------- emission\n   445\t;; 6a's emit-node with install children numbered by discovery order and a\n   446\t;; row path threaded for the child refusal.\n   447\t\n   448\t(def ^:private ordered-slots @#'rows/ordered-slots)\n   449\t(def ^:private check-literal-markers! @#'rows/check-literal-markers!)\n   450\t(def ^:private byte-compare @#'rows/byte-compare)\n\n**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\nrow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\nkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\nspecifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is\nnot entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a\n**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering\nat the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that\none numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\nequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\norder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\ncontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\nan entry's key before its value.\n\n**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\ncell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\ncounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\nlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\nenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\nno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\ncontents table.\n\n**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\nre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\nthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\nre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\ncell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\nit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)\njoins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is\none worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred\ngroups from every depth.\n\n**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\nlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\ncell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\nfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\nso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component\nby a component run over the synthetic ordered row\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral\nsequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that\ngroup, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a\nsynthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be\ncomplete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it\nreaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the\ncomponents by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:\neach of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,\nin that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's\nnumbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,\nlemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).\nRemove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group\nwhose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\ncarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\nhost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n\n**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when\nit holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the\nroot run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component\nrun of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed\n`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered\ncursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the\n1bb2638cfcf00e509f34bf463e3083e51d593697\nd77da9f04276fca34db6fe6c36bea38a576c09b2\n67 449 physical 383 nonblank noncomment 337\n129 407 physical 279 nonblank noncomment 251\n34 449 physical 416 nonblank noncomment 361\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
